From 1d3e8cd74fb5edce32b6bb3aec2263db4edb229b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DeepSeek Harness <noreply@deepseek.com>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 22:27:11 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Stop calling the Windows core a stub in the build file

Two comments still described the tree as it was before the port landed, and
both of them are the kind of stale documentation that is worse than none:
they tell a reader the Windows core reports "unsupported" and does nothing.

CMakeLists.txt: the file header still said the Windows core is
core/win/livehime-core-stub.cpp. The WIN32 branch has compiled
core/win/livehime-core.cpp for the whole port; only the header was left
behind. It now names the real core and points at the two documents that
describe it.

livehime-core-stub.cpp: its header still ended with "Replace function by
function as the port proceeds; delete this file when the last one is gone."
The port is finished and the file is deliberately kept as the record of the
state the first Windows build ran in, so the header now says so.

Comment-only: no translation unit changes, so the released v0.2.10 binaries
are unaffected.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 plugins/livehime/CMakeLists.txt               | 13 ++++++++----
 .../livehime/core/win/livehime-core-stub.cpp  | 20 ++++++++++---------
 2 files changed, 20 insertions(+), 13 deletions(-)

diff --git a/plugins/livehime/CMakeLists.txt b/plugins/livehime/CMakeLists.txt
index c99c058..917758b 100644
--- a/plugins/livehime/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/plugins/livehime/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -17,11 +17,16 @@ find_package(Qt6 REQUIRED Widgets)
 # per platform instead of assuming Darwin.
 #
 #   macOS    core = Swift (core/Sources/LiveHimeCore), window-native.mm
-#   Windows  core = core/win/livehime-core-stub.cpp,  window-native.cpp
+#   Windows  core = C++   (core/win/livehime-core.cpp),  window-native.cpp
 #
-# The Windows core is a stub: it implements the whole livehime-core.h C ABI so
-# the plugin compiles, links and loads, and reports "unsupported" to the UI.
-# Porting the real core is tracked in docs/WINDOWS_PORT.md.
+# The Windows core is a real C++ port of the Swift one, not a stub: it implements
+# the whole livehime-core.h C ABI against WinHTTP, BCrypt, the Credential
+# Manager, WIC and WebView2. The first Windows build compiled against
+# core/win/livehime-core-stub.cpp, which is kept in the tree as the record of
+# that state and is no longer part of the build.
+#
+# What the port covers, and what it does not: docs/WINDOWS_PORT.md.
+# The frozen interfaces: docs/port-spec/CONTRACT.md.
 # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
 
 # Portable sources: identical on every platform.
diff --git a/plugins/livehime/core/win/livehime-core-stub.cpp b/plugins/livehime/core/win/livehime-core-stub.cpp
index 4b16770..2833e2d 100644
--- a/plugins/livehime/core/win/livehime-core-stub.cpp
+++ b/plugins/livehime/core/win/livehime-core-stub.cpp
@@ -1,20 +1,22 @@
-/* livehime-core-stub.cpp — Windows build of the LiveHime session core.
+/* livehime-core-stub.cpp — the first Windows build's session core. SUPERSEDED.
  *
- * The real core lives in core/Sources/LiveHimeCore/*.swift and is macOS-only:
- * it imports WebKit, AppKit, Security, CryptoKit, AVFoundation, Speech,
- * ImageIO, UniformTypeIdentifiers, CoreImage and Compression, with no
- * `#if os(...)` fallback anywhere (see docs/WINDOWS_PORT.md).
+ * Not built. core/win/livehime-core.cpp is the real Windows core and is what
+ * CMakeLists.txt compiles; this file is kept as the record of the state the
+ * first Windows build ran in, while the port landed file by file behind it.
+ *
+ * Why it had to exist: the upstream core lives in
+ * core/Sources/LiveHimeCore/*.swift and is macOS-only — it imports WebKit,
+ * AppKit, Security, CryptoKit, AVFoundation, Speech, ImageIO,
+ * UniformTypeIdentifiers, CoreImage and Compression, with no `#if os(...)`
+ * fallback anywhere (see docs/WINDOWS_PORT.md).
  *
  * This file implements the entire livehime-core.h C ABI so the Qt UI compiles,
- * links and loads on Windows, and so the port can land file by file behind it.
+ * links and loads on Windows.
  *
  * Contract with the UI: every entry point reports
  * {"state":"failed","event":...} / {"status":"unsupported"} through the state
  * callback. Nothing is ever reported as success. The UI already handles these
  * shapes — it must never show "signed in" or "live" on the strength of a stub.
- *
- * Replace function by function as the port proceeds; delete this file when the
- * last one is gone.
  */
 
 #include "livehime-core.h"
-- 
